    "question": {
      ":question/id": "a7a3b533-c647-5216-897a-595c97dc4c2a",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "$\\int 8x(4{x}^{2}-4)^{8}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We note that $8x$ is the derivative of the quantity in the parentheses. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu=4{x}^{2}-4.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =8x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=8x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 8x(4{x}^{2}-4)^{8}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4{x}^{2}-4)^{8}\\,8x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{8}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\int {u}^{8}\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{{u}^{9}}{9}+C\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute $u=4{x}^{2}-4$ back in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\int 8x(4{x}^{2}-4)^{8}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{(4{x}^{2}-4)^{9}}{9}+C.\n$$",
