      ":question/problem": "$\\int 5{x}^{4}({x}^{5}-6)^{10}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We note that $5{x}^{4}$ is the derivative of the quantity in the parentheses. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu={x}^{5}-6.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =5{x}^{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=5{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 5{x}^{4}({x}^{5}-6)^{10}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({x}^{5}-6)^{10}\\,5{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{10}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\int {u}^{10}\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{{u}^{11}}{11}+C\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute $u={x}^{5}-6$ back in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\int 5{x}^{4}({x}^{5}-6)^{10}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{({x}^{5}-6)^{11}}{11}+C.\n$$",
