      ":question/worked-solution": "We note that $8x$ is the derivative of the quantity in the square root. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu=4{x}^{2}-4.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =8x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=8x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 8x\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-4}\\,8x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{{u}^{3/2}}{(\\frac{3}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute $u=4{x}^{2}-4$ back in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\int 8x\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{2(4{x}^{2}-4)^{3/2}}{3}+C.\n$$",
